WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:05.430
Thank you to Draper and its Hack the Moon
initiative for supporting PBS Digital Studios.

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:09.530
The red planet has captured our attention
throughout human history.

00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:14.389
With its relative proximity to us and to the
sun, early astronomers thought it was possible

00:00:14.389 --> 00:00:16.090
for life to exist on Mars.

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:21.630
We’ve imagined martians as everything from
humans with silver antennae, to skeletal invaders

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:22.910
with exposed brains.

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:28.230
From a helmeted cartoon to muscular, four-armed
bipedal warriors.

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:31.189
Annddd whatever this thing this.

00:00:31.189 --> 00:00:39.350
PBS even has its own Martians.

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:43.240
Some martians are depicted as monsters, while
others are allies.

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:45.680
Why are they all so different?

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:49.590
Technology plays a role, and so does biology,
natural history, and war.

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:56.870
But the whole martian craze began with just
a single mistranslated word.

00:00:56.870 --> 00:01:03.350
[Monstrum Intro]

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:09.390
The modern history of Mars began in 1877 when
a crucial discovery was made: a network of

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:11.980
lines were visible on the surface.

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:15.870
The Italian astronomer who discovered these
grooves named them “canali.”

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:16.870
“Channels” in English.

00:01:16.870 --> 00:01:23.810
In 1895, astronomer Percival Lowell published
the first illustrated book on Mars.

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:28.820
Lowell observed the same lines, and writing
for an English-speaking audience, translated

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:33.430
the word canali to “canals” rather than
“channels.”

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:37.900
And that’s when a fateful mistranslation
changed Mars history forever.

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:42.140
He speculated how life could survive there
if it did indeed exist.

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:46.710
But then, Lowell’s theories got a little
out there: His book claimed that the surface

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:52.280
markings visible on Mars were deliberate as
quote: “no natural theory has yet been advanced

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:54.520
which will explain these lines.”

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:59.890
He theorized that what he was seeing were
changes in vegetation made possible by a vast

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:01.810
series of irrigation canals.

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:07.200
And even though he insists that he is only
referring to the possibility of an intelligent

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:12.969
lifeform on Mars, he also says “Certainly
what we see hints at the existence of beings

00:02:12.969 --> 00:02:18.269
who are in advance of, not behind us, in the
journey of life.”

00:02:18.269 --> 00:02:22.960
Now that a scientist had suggested intelligent
life on Mars, the public couldn’t get the

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:26.990
idea out of their heads, and the press had
a field day.

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:33.659
Newspapers and magazines reprinted Lowell’s
maps, adding a visual element to Mars mythology.

00:02:33.659 --> 00:02:38.730
Depictions in literature of the time presented
Martians as humanoid in form-and their society

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:39.730
utopian.

00:02:39.730 --> 00:02:45.499
No war, no poverty, no social classes, no
prisons, and no alcoholism.

00:02:45.499 --> 00:02:49.519
Martians were portrayed as advanced beings
both in technology and intelligence.

00:02:49.519 --> 00:02:53.609
They were odd, but kind of appealing.

00:02:53.609 --> 00:02:55.140
Until The War of the Worlds that is.

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:57.609
No not that one.

00:02:57.609 --> 00:02:59.680
Nope, not that one
either.

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:00.680
Further back.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:04.319
Yes, this one.

00:03:04.319 --> 00:03:09.520
Not only did H.G. Wells give us one of the
first alien invasion novels of all time, his

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:12.579
Martians were very far from human.

00:03:12.579 --> 00:03:15.549
Although they did possess a superior intelligence.

00:03:15.549 --> 00:03:20.709
They also wanted to kill humans—and inject
themselves with our blood.

00:03:20.709 --> 00:03:26.129
Initially serialized in Pearson’s Magazine
in 1897, the story was collected and published

00:03:26.129 --> 00:03:30.569
as one volume in 1898 and was an immediate
success.

00:03:30.569 --> 00:03:32.580
It has never been out of print.

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:38.799
It increased in popularity after Orson Welles’
infamous radio broadcast in 1938.

00:03:38.799 --> 00:03:43.000
On October 30, listeners tuned in to hear
his dramatic reading of the book.

00:03:57.779 --> 00:04:02.969
The show was so realistic he managed to convince
some people Martians were actually invading

00:04:02.969 --> 00:04:04.859
New Jersey.

00:04:04.859 --> 00:04:09.459
Other than my beloved Yip Yips, and Marvin
the Martian, these were the first aliens I

00:04:09.459 --> 00:04:13.370
was ever exposed to, and they left a lasting
impression.

00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:14.819
How could they not?

00:04:14.819 --> 00:04:19.910
After all H.G. Wells’ aliens were essentially
giant heads about 4 feet in diameter with

00:04:19.910 --> 00:04:26.810
no nostrils, a fleshy beak, large eyes, and
sixteen tentacles arranged in two bunches.

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:30.360
Their internal structure was basically all
brain.

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:35.390
What was so scary about these Martians was
their advanced war machinery.

00:04:35.390 --> 00:04:40.691
Wells describes a “monstrous tripod” 100-feet-high
capable of traveling with the speed “of

00:04:40.691 --> 00:04:42.370
an express train.”

00:04:42.370 --> 00:04:48.280
These machines, driven by the Martians, have
flexible metal tentacles, heat-ray guns, and

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:52.860
a chemical weapon in the form of “black
smoke.”

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:53.860
See?

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:54.860
Terrifying.

00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:57.810
In what is regarded as one of the best plot
twists in science fiction, the Martians are

00:04:57.810 --> 00:05:02.860
destroyed not by human military or weapons,
but by bacteria.

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:09.020
While serious, persistent criticism of the
supposed canals on Mars began in the 20th

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:13.760
century, that did not stop authors from writing
about life on the red planet.

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:19.290
Some authors latched on to Mars as an uncharted
frontier ripe for nostalgic romance driven

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:24.570
by masculinist fantasy, a place for swords
and magic, beasts and treasure, slave-girls,

00:05:24.570 --> 00:05:30.980
princesses, and other beautiful female Martians
just dying to be loved by a human man

00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:34.520
Even film picked up on the trend

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:37.690
Following World War I, we see a shift in Martians.

00:05:37.690 --> 00:05:43.080
The human explorers traveling to Mars in these
texts were met with menace, and we see a surge

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:49.360
of non-mammalian extraterrestrials similar
to Earthly insects, reptiles, or worms that

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:52.780
were, more often than not, predators.

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:57.860
Stories of aliens from this period could be
read as American anxieties about nationalism

00:05:57.860 --> 00:05:59.860
and racial segregation.

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:04.010
When Martians were described as more humanoid,
they were still a threat because of their

00:06:04.010 --> 00:06:05.550
advanced weaponry.

00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:09.310
It wasn’t really until after World War II
that Martians were more commonly referred

00:06:09.310 --> 00:06:10.550
to as “aliens.”

00:06:10.550 --> 00:06:16.160
This word alien was specifically linked with
a new term and concept for the time—the

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:18.700
“unidentified flying object.”

00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:23.730
As consumer cameras and recording devices
became more accessible, so did the popularity

00:06:23.730 --> 00:06:25.240
of UFOs.

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:29.390
People could now capture and share “alien”
experiences.

00:06:29.390 --> 00:06:33.980
Martian literature post-WWII reflected a desire
to escape Earth.

00:06:33.980 --> 00:06:39.180
To flee from repressive governments, nuclear
weapons, and other human-created evils.

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:44.000
Like in The Martian Chronicles, where humans
flee Earth for various reasons including nuclear

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:46.040
war, government censorship, and racism.

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:53.200
As its author, Ray Bradbury once famously
said, “Mars is a mirror, not a crystal.”

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:58.510
As our science and technology advanced, and
the idea of life on Mars was largely debunked,

00:06:58.510 --> 00:07:02.700
a belief in Martians became even more of a
fantasy.

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:07.950
After the invention of in-space telescopic
photography, and the Mariner 9 and Viking

00:07:07.950 --> 00:07:12.490
Missions, people pretty much gave up hope
that above ground organisms lived on Mars.

00:07:12.490 --> 00:07:20.660
Of course this hasn’t stopped people from
creating stories about them.

00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:24.680
Mars is more visible and more accessible to
us than ever.

00:07:24.680 --> 00:07:29.690
While many still hold out for the possibility
of life on Mars buried deep below its crust

00:07:29.690 --> 00:07:36.240
or in the polar ice caps, we are more concerned
with trying to put humans on Mars than encountering

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:39.000
some kind of life form there.

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:41.890
Today, narratives about Martians have changed.

00:07:41.890 --> 00:07:47.590
We’ve moved away from imagining Mars as
a pre-existing utopia, populated by a Martian

00:07:47.590 --> 00:07:49.540
race superior to our own.

00:07:49.540 --> 00:07:55.900
Now, we envision Mars as a utopia in-waiting,
a promise of hope and a better life, off-planet

00:07:55.900 --> 00:07:57.160
for Earthlings.

00:07:57.160 --> 00:08:02.970
PBS is bringing you the universe with SUMMER
OF SPACE, which includes six incredible new

00:08:02.970 --> 00:08:09.350
science and history shows streaming on PBS.org
and the PBS Video app, like The Planets from

00:08:09.350 --> 00:08:10.350
NOVA.

00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:11.350
There are also many celestial episodes from
PBS Digital Studios creators!

00:08:11.350 --> 00:08:15.750
